The '''Ampurias Tower''' ({{Lang-bl|Torre Ampurias}}), and previously known as the '''Gran Castejón Tower''' ({{lang-bl|Torre Gran Castejón}}) is a 128-story, {{convert|625|m|ft|adj=mid|sp=us}}-tall [[W:Megatall skyscraper|megatall skyscraper]] in [[Ampurias]], [[Castejón]], and the main building of the [[Castejón International Financial Center]] complex. It is popularly known as ''El Molí'' ("the windmill") because of the 270 vertical-axis [[W:Wind turbine|wind turbines]] located in the facade and near the top of the tower. It is the tallest building in Balisca, [[Abyala]], and the Northern and Western hemispheres by architectural height. The Ampurias Tower also possesses the world's largest {{W|tuned mass damper}}.

It is third [[List of tallest buildings in the world|tallest building by height to architectural top]] in Adonia (after the BUILDING IN FENIA and BUILDING IN ZONG) and it shares the record (along with XXX) of having Adonia's highest [[W:Observation deck|observation deck]] within a building or structure at {{convert|562|m|ft|adj=mid|sp=us}}. 

== History ==

=== Planning, funding and approval ===
The project was first conceptualized in 2008 during the tenure of [[Cristiano Sandoval]] as a symbol of Balisca's "re-engagement" with the global economy following the post-Cold War era. The tower was proposed by the [[Salto Bay Authority]], a public-benefit corporation, to serve as the headquarters of [[Sifr]], a Baliscan [[W:Technology company|tech company]], and a hub for international trade unions and [[W:Cooperative federation|cooperative federations]].

To align with Balisca's strict environmental laws and the "soft energy path," the architects [[Lamar Torres]] and [[Santi Jacintha]] designed the building to be "energy positive." The design incorporated a unique aerodynamic shape to funnel wind into 270 integrated vertical-axis turbines, theoretically allowing the building to generate 15% more electricity than it consumes. The groundbreaking ceremony occurred in 2010.

==== Public opposition ====
The announcement of the Ampurias Tower triggered immediate and militant backlash from powerful anti-capitalist and anarchist factions, who viewed the megatall structure as an act of "excessive vanity architecture" and a symbol of the continued concentration of wealth in Castejón. Opposition was spearheaded by the radical environmentalist front [[Ecological Action]] and cells of the [[Desconexión|''desconexión'']] subculture, who cited the Baliscan legal principle of ''bien fortificado'' ("battle-ready democracy") to justify physical resistance against the project.

Protests rapidly escalated from [[W:Civil disobedience|civil disobedience]] to [[W:Illegalism|illegalism]] and sabotage. In what became known as the "Siege of Ampurias" in 2011, masked ''desconexiónistas'' and anarchist affinity groups breached the construction perimeter, disabling heavy machinery and establishing a [[W:Zone to Defend|zone to defend]] (ZAD) on the foundation site. They engaged in ''[[W:Détournement|détournement]]'' tactics, hijacking the project's promotional billboards to display anti-consumerist slogans and jamming the Salto Bay Authority’s digital communications.

The conflict resulted in violent clashes with the ''[[Mozos de Escuadra]]'' and private security contractors. The construction was delayed for nearly two years due to persistent arson attacks on supply trucks and the "concrete sabotage" campaign, where protesters mixed sugar into cement shipments to ruin the foundation pours. The Salto Bay Authority was ultimately forced to negotiate a "Peace Accord" with the activist coalitions, which included the binding agreement to convert the building's base into a public commons and to hand over operational management of the wind turbines to a local energy cooperative.

=== 2018 and later ===
The building was completed on 8 June 2018 and opened to the public on 7 May 2019. Upon opening, it struggled to reach full occupancy. While [[Sifr]], [[Através]], and several major [[Essential industry|state-owned enterprises]] moved in, the upper floors—intended for regional headquaters of multinational companies and luxury hotel brands—remained largely vacant, due to the cooling global economy and Balisca's restrictive business regulations.

==== COVID-19 pandemic and conversion ====
{{See also|Casajuan Plan}}The outbreak of the [[W:COVID-19 pandemic|COVID-19 pandemic]] in 2020 left the tower nearly empty as Baliscan society rapidly shifted towards [[W:Remote work|remote work]]. The subsequent [[2021 Baliscan civil unrest]] brought the building back into the spotlight. During the July protests, demonstrators occupied the lobby and ransacked many of the lower floors, while simultaneously demanding the utilization of the empty space to address the city's critical housing shortage.

In response, the new administration of [[Zavier Bilal]] and the Secretariat of Local Administration designated the Ampurias Tower as a pilot project for the [[Casajuan Plan|Urban Redevelopment Project]], popularly known as the "Casajuan Plan".

Between 2022 and 2024, floors 40 through 110 were retrofitted and converted from commercial office space into limited-equity [[W:Housing cooperatives|housing cooperatives]]. Today, the tower functions as a "vertical commune," housing over 8,000 residents. It contains:

* '''Floors 1-20:''' Public vertical gardens, markets and transit connections.
* '''Floors 21-39:''' Offices of [[Sifr]], [[Através]] and the Salto Bay Authority.
* '''Floors 40-110:''' Cooperative residential units (''Cooperativa de Vivienda Torre Ampurias'').
* '''Floors 111-128:''' Atmospheric research stations and the public observation deck.

The successful conversion of the tower has been cited by the [[Secretariat of Local Administration and Modernization (Balisca)|Secretariat of Local Administration and Modernization]] as a model for sustainable density and the adaptive reuse of infrastructure.

